{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head a15eeb6f0568a564e0532665f2d336f4e84e16cc (origin/request/mcp-per-call-actor-selector-recut, one commit on current main 526b0816, Rests-On request #15661), fulfilling review request #15844 against all nineteen cited path artifacts. Gates reproduced in a detached checkout of this head: go vet ./... clean, go build ./... clean, go test -count=1 -race ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/... ok (119.6s), go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... ok. The request's conditions are met in substance: per-call repo and agent selectors with startup defaults preserved; empty or non-string selectors refuse (main.go selectedActor/selectedRepo); validateSelection loads the existing key, refuses a mismatched fingerprint, and confirms live participant standing per selected repository through the resident orientation or the verified local roster, with no path back to the startup identity; docs and SKILL.md state that key access is the trust boundary and that name-derived development keys are not yet a real gate. Three changes are requested before approval. (1) Per-call cost: call() runs app.Open and validateSelection on every tool call, and app.Open walks the entire first-parent sequence log for the binding check, so each call, including every iteration of a wait loop, streams the whole log (about 16 MB at this workroom's depth) plus six extra git subprocesses per resident-mode call measured with a PATH shim against main (56 to 69 and 78 to 108 subprocesses on two existing tests); heartbeat repeats the validation for every attended room every ten seconds. The comment calls this a precedence check rather than a cached authorization, which is right for the advertisement and the key, but the binding walk is O(depth) and grows with the log. Validate the binding once per (repo, agent, fingerprint) and re-check only the advertisement and the key file per durable act, or cache the binding by ref position; keep the no-fallback refusals. (2) Dead